      "title": "CUPE - AR - Site Web -Département FRA - Automne 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: Replacement of a temporary position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: Faculty: Unit: Département de français_Students and Casuals Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Course Title: Course Code: Section: Supervisor name (if known): Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/18 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/02 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Préparation / Preparation:0 Contact avec étudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: Work Start Date: September 18, 2026 Work End Date: December 31, 2026 Total Work Hours: 0 Hourly Rate: Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: In person Language of Work: Requirements and Nature of Work: Exigences minimum: Baccalauréat avec spécialisation en lettres françaises ou dans un domaine équivalent. Excellente maîtrise du français écrit. Avoir complété les formations requises par l’université pour l’accès aux sites internet ou avoir la disponibilité pour les suivre à courte échéance. Expérience préalable dans la rédaction de communications professionnelles et en création de contenu et gestion de réseau sociaux. Le comité de sélection se réserve le droit de retenir en priorité les candidatures qui répondront étroitement au critère de l’excellence linguistique. Description d'emploi Le ou la responsable des réseaux sociaux et du site internet du Département de français aura pour tâche principale de faire rayonner la vie départementale et d’assurer la liaison entre tous les membres de la vaste communauté littéraire francophone. Tâches principales: Contribuer de façon régulière à la mise à jour de la page web du Département de français (recherche comparative, conception d’une modélisation, rédaction de textes, ajout de matériel, etc.) Animer les réseaux sociaux du Département de français par la publication hebdomadaire de nouvelles et d’annonces ; Préparer des affiches pour des activités et annonces du Département de français ; Assurer la bonne diffusion des annonces et nouvelles auprès de l’ensemble des membres du Département de français ; Diffuser des annonces et nouvelles du Département de français auprès des membres de la Faculté des arts ou de la vaste communauté universitaire ; Participer à des discussions de planification avec le comité de direction du Département de français. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    },
    "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Lees-Campus/CUPE-Automne-2026-SSP3900-TA-120h_JR39135-1": {
      "title": "CUPE_Automne 2026_SSP3900_TA_120h",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Faculté des sciences de la santé / Faculty of Health Sciences Unit: Direction, Interdisciplinary School of Health Sciences_ST Job Classification: Teaching Assistant/Demonstrator/Lab Monitor (CUPE) Posting Type: Cours précis / Specific course Course Title: Cours auto-dirigés – Automne 2026 Course Code: SSP3900 Section: Supervisor name (if known): Maryse Sullivan Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/17 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/25 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Préparation / Preparation:0 Contact avec étudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: 25 Work Start Date: September 25, 2026 Work End Date: December 31, 2026 Total Work Hours: 120 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $31.89 ou/or $50.25 (2024-2025) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: En ligne Language of Work: Anglais | English, Français | French Requirements and Nature of Work: A) Cote et description du cours SSP 3900 Cours autodirigé d’apprentissage expérientiel (3 crédits) Réalisation, individuelle ou en petit groupe, d’une activité expérientielle autodirigée, encadrée par les principes de l’apprentissage expérientiel et supervisée par un professeur. Les étudiantes et étudiants de la Faculté des sciences de la santé seront amenés à réfléchir sur les compétences transversales, notamment les uOCompétences, acquises durant l’activité expérientielle tout en tissant des liens avec les apprentissages académiques de leur parcours. B) Dates des cours 15 septembre 2026 au 31 décembre 2026 C) Nature du travail, responsabilités et compétences requises pour le poste Les cours auto-dirigés constituent une nouvelle formule d’apprentissage expérientiel numérique à l’Université d’Ottawa, alignée sur le cadre uOCompétences et inspirée du modèle SLICC à l’Université d’Édimbourg. Chaque assistant·e d’enseignement (AE) jouera un rôle clé d’accompagnateur·trice auprès d’un groupe d’étudiant·e·s, les guidant dans la planification, la mise en œuvre et la réflexion critique de leur projet respectif. Ce rôle est de nature principalement mentorale et formative. Une expérience à superviser des étudiant.e.s dans le cadre d’un cours autodirigé est essentielle. Le ou la candidat.e doit faire preuve d’un haut degré d’autonomie et être capable de travailler de manière indépendante dans un environnement d’apprentissage flexible et centré sur l’étudiant.e. Tâches principales 1. Correction et rétroaction (≈ 85 heures) Corriger les travaux, devoirs et évaluations déposés sur Brightspace. Fournir une rétroaction claire, structurée et constructive. 2. Contact étudiant (≈ 15 heures) Répondre aux questions des étudiants par courriel, les rencontrer en ligne au besoin. Offrir un soutien académique de première ligne selon les directives de la professeure. 3. Rencontres avec la professeure et formations initiales (≈ 10 heures) Participer aux réunions de coordination et de correction. Suivre les formations obligatoires 4. Ateliers Zoom durant la session (≈ 10 heures) Assister aux séances synchrones. Offrir un soutien technique ou pédagogique en temps réel. Profil recherché Étudiant·e aux cycles supérieurs (sciences de la santé ou domaine connexe). Parfait bilinguisme : excellente communication écrite et orale en français et en anglais. Autonomie, rigueur et bonne gestion du temps. Expérience en correction et en enseignement faisant emploi des outils numériques (Brightspace, Zoom, MS Teams). Bonne compréhension du fonctionnement des cours autodirigés à uOttawa. Bonne compréhension de l’apprentissage expérientiel ou du cadre uOCompétences. Expérience dans des rôles de mentorat, de tutorat, en COOP ou au sein du développement de carrière à uOttawa (un atout). D) Mode de livraison  Le cours est offert entièrement en ligne, principalement en mode asynchrone. Quelques séances synchrones sur Zoom sont prévues au cours du trimestre. L’enseignement et les communications sont bilingues, selon les besoins des étudiantes et étudiants. E) Nombre total d’heures du contrat : 120 heures Tâches Correction 85 h Contacts étudiants 15 h Rencontres + formations 10 h Ateliers Zoom 10 h Total du contrat 120 h ***** A) Course Code SSP 3900 Experiential Learning Self-directed (3 units) Completion, individually or in small groups, of a self-directed experiential activity that is guided by the principles of experiential learning, supervised by a professor. Students of the Faculty of Health Sciences will reflect on the transferable competencies, in particular the uOCompetencies, acquired during the experiential activity while making connections to their academic studies. B) Course Dates September 15, 2026 to December 31, 2026 C) Nature of the work, responsibilities, and skills required for the position Self-directed courses are a new form of experiential learning at the University of Ottawa, aligned with the uOcompetencies framework and inspired by the SLICC model at the University of Edinburgh. Each teaching assistant (TA) will play a key role as a mentor to a group of students, guiding them in the planning, implementation, and critical reflection of their projects. This role is primarily mentoring and formative in nature. Experience supervising students in a self-directed course is essential. Candidates must demonstrate a high degree of autonomy, and the ability to work independently in a flexible and student-centred learning environment. Main responsibilities: 1. Grading and feedback (≈ 85 hours) Grade assignments, homework, and assessments submitted on Brightspace. Provide clear, structured, and constructive feedback. 2. Student contact (≈ 15 hours) Respond to student questions by email, meet with students online as needed. Provide front-line academic support according to the instructor's guidelines. 3. Meetings with the professor and initial training (≈ 10 hours) Participate in coordination and marking meetings. Complete mandatory training. 4. Zoom workshops during the semester (≈ 10 hours) Attend synchronous sessions. Provide real-time technical or pedagogical support. Skills required: Graduate student (health sciences or related field). Fully bilingual: excellent written and oral communication skills in French and English. Ability to work independently, attention to detail, and good time management skills. Experience in grading and teaching using digital tools (Brightspace, Zoom, MS Teams). Good understanding of how self-directed courses work at uOttawa. Good understanding of experiential learning or the uOCompetencies framework. Experience in mentoring, tutoring, CO-OP programs, or career development at uOttawa (an asset). D) Course Delivery  The course is offered entirely online, mainly in asynchronous format. A few synchronous sessions on Zoom are planned during the semester. Teaching and communications are bilingual, depending on the needs of the students. E) Total number of hours in the contract: 120 hours Tasks Marking 85 hrs Student Contact 15 hrs Meetings and training 10 hrs Zoom Workshops 10 hrs Contact Total 120 hrs Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 – Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."
    }
